const faq = [
  { q: 'How many common ravens are there in the world?', a: 'Global Common Raven population estimates are difficult to make precisely due to the species\' vast range, but BirdLife International estimates the population at approximately 16 million individuals (mature birds), with some estimates ranging higher. The species has a stable to expanding population trend in most of its range. It is classified as Least Concern by the IUCN — the lowest conservation concern category. Localized declines exist in some regions, but global population is robust.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens increasing or decreasing in population?', a: 'Common Ravens have been expanding in range and increasing in population in many parts of their range since the mid-20th century, primarily due to: legal protection (ravens were widely persecuted as livestock predators until the 20th century; legal protection in the UK came only in 1954); increasing food availability from human waste (landfills, refuse sites, tourism); and adaptation to suburban and urban environments. In some regions (Mojave Desert, California), raven populations have expanded dramatically due to human infrastructure providing water and food — with negative consequences for desert tortoises and other species through nest predation.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens endangered?', a: 'No — the Common Raven is not endangered. It is classified as Least Concern by the IUCN with an estimated population of approximately 16 million birds. Some other raven species have more restricted ranges: the Thick-billed Raven is endemic to the Ethiopian highlands; the Somali Crow has the most restricted range of any raven. However, none of the eight raven species are currently classified as threatened. The myth that ravens are endangered likely stems from their historical persecution and local rarity in some areas.' },
];
